//! LiberSystem process package - process loading and job control.

package liber:process@1;

use liber:base@1.{error, koid};
use liber:resources@1.{budget};

/// One process the ProcessService has started: the kernel object id of the new
/// process and the init-package entry name it was launched from.
record process-info {
	koid: koid,
	name: string,
}

/// A live process the launcher drives - the kernel Process object handed back as a
/// capability (carrying MANAGE), so the caller can do job control on the program it
/// started: signal it and wait on it. Named `task` to stay distinct from the `process`
/// service interface; it is the same kernel Process the koid names.
///
/// `@kernel(process)` is what makes that last sentence checkable: a `@rights` guard on a
/// `handle<task>` now refuses a handle of any other kernel object, rather than checking the rights
/// on whatever it was handed.
@kernel(process)
resource task;

/// A live ProcessGroup - every stage of a pipeline under one object, so signalling and waiting
/// reach all of them at once.
///
/// A TYPE OF ITS OWN, and not because a group is unlike a process to the kernel. `handle<task>`
/// used to name both, and every record that could carry either carried a boolean beside it saying
/// which - so the two could disagree, and nothing on the wire could tell. Signalling and waiting
/// take a different syscall for each, so a mismatched pair is a signal sent to the wrong kind of
/// object, which is exactly the case a type is for. `@kernel(process-group)` is that distinction
/// enforced at the boundary instead of described above it.
@kernel(process-group)
resource task-group;

/// The result of launching a program: the live process as a capability (transferred
/// out-of-band as handle<task>) plus its process-info (koid + name). The handle is the
/// launcher's job-control handle on the started program.
record start-result {
	task: handle<task>,
	info: process-info,
}

/// EVERY LIMIT A DOMAIN HAS, ALL OF THEM FINITE.
///
/// `launch-bounded` takes one number and leaves the other five at no limit, which is what a tool
/// launched to do one thing needs: the resource it could exhaust is memory and the rest are bounded
/// by what a short-lived process can reach. A SERVICE IS NOT THAT SHAPE. It runs for the life of the
/// boot, it holds channels to other services, and the ways it can grow without bound are handles it
/// never closes, threads it keeps creating and queued messages nobody drains - none of which a
/// memory ceiling notices until the machine is already in trouble.
///
/// SO EVERY FIELD IS REQUIRED AND NONE OF THEM MAY BE UNLIMITED. A record with a field left at
/// `u64::MAX` is refused rather than accepted as "no opinion about that one": the whole point of
/// this record is that a service's footprint is stated, and a field nobody filled in is a limit
/// nobody decided.
record resource-limits {
	/// Bytes of memory the Domain may have mapped.
	memory:    u64,
	/// Handles it may hold at once.
	handles:   u64,
	/// Threads it may run.
	threads:   u64,
	/// Bytes of queued IPC it may have outstanding.
	ipc-queue: u64,
	/// Bytes of thread stack.
	stack:     u64,
	/// Bytes of DMA-capable memory. ZERO IS A REAL VALUE AND IS THE ORDINARY ONE HERE: a service
	/// with no device claim has no business addressing a device's memory, and saying so costs
	/// nothing where the alternative is a number nobody chose.
	dma:       u64,
}

/// The Process service contract: the process-loading mechanism. `start` launches an
/// unattended program (no bootstrap capability), `list` reports the processes started
/// so far, and `launch` is the full launcher primitive - it loads and starts a program
/// with a caller-provided bootstrap channel (so a policy front end like PermissionManager
/// can grant the new process its capabilities over that channel) and returns the live
/// process handle for job control. `launch-bounded` is the same mechanism under a child
/// Domain created for that one process: the limit is what that process may use, not a budget
/// shared with whatever else asked for the same number, and the Domain is released when the
/// process it accounts ends. The policy front end chooses the limit, while
/// ProcessService remains responsible only for loading. The service holds no grantable
/// clients and decides no capability grants.
///
/// `accounting` reports the live budget of every Domain this service currently accounts -
/// one per running launch. It exists because a per-launch Domain is invisible to
/// ResourceManager, which can only report the Domains it was handed, so isolation was
/// enforced and unobservable. Reporting rather than delegating is the point: a Domain
/// handle sent to an observer would outlive the process it accounts until the observer
/// learned that it ended, which is the lifetime problem a Domain-per-launch removed. This
/// answers with values, transfers no handle, and grants nothing.
///
/// `launch-prepared` and `release` are the start gate a pipeline needs: every stage of
/// `a | b | c` must exist and have its stream endpoints installed before ANY of them runs, or
/// an early stage writes into a consumer that has not been handed its reader yet. A prepared
/// launch is a loaded process whose first thread exists and has not been queued; `release`
/// queues it. The caller gets the same `start-result` an ordinary launch returns, so job
/// control is identical - what differs is only that the program has not begun.
///
/// The start token itself never leaves this service, and a prepared launch is keyed by the CHANNEL
/// it was prepared on as well as its koid - so naming another client's koid answers exactly as
/// naming one that does not exist, which is the truth from where that caller stands.
///
/// A move-only handle would be the other way to say that, and it does not fit: `release-group` takes
/// one token per stage and a message carries at most `abi::MAX_MESSAGE_CAPS` (four) capabilities,
/// while a pipeline may have eight stages. A token per stage would make the all-or-none group
/// release - the thing `security.run-pipeline` promises - unexpressible.
///
/// `cancel` is the other end of `release`: it drops a prepared launch and tears the loaded process
/// down. Without it the only ways out of a prepared launch were to start it or to leave it - and
/// PermissionManager has several early returns that abandon a pipeline midway, which left a process
/// loaded, stopped, and holding its Domain forever. A client that simply GOES is cancelled too: the
/// service is told when a client's channel closes and abandons everything that client prepared.
///
/// `release-group` is the same gate for a WHOLE pipeline, and it exists because releasing one koid
/// at a time cannot keep the promise `security.run-pipeline` makes. Past the first release a
/// transaction can no longer be unwound: a stage that has run may already have written, sent or
/// printed, so telling it to stop afterwards is not the same as never having started it, and "a
/// failure at any stage starts none of them" was false exactly when it mattered.
///
/// So this validates EVERY token first - every koid prepared, and every one of them this client's -
/// and only then queues them. A caller naming one koid it does not own starts nothing at all, which
/// is the transition the guarantee needs and which no sequence of single releases can provide.
/// Queueing after validation is a local operation on threads that already exist; a failure there is
/// a fault rather than a policy answer, and it is reported as an error rather than as `false`.
///
/// `release` answers the same three ways, and a caller that has granted a program its capabilities
/// needs all three: `true` started it; `false` is a PRE-START REFUSAL - no pending launch of the
/// caller's by that koid, nothing ran, nothing to cancel; and an error is a POST-REMOVAL START
/// FAILURE - the token was taken and the kernel refused to queue the thread, so the launch cannot be
/// cancelled afterwards and this service forgets the record and its Domain itself. A group release
/// that reports an error has forgotten every member that failed to start the same way; the ones
/// that started are running, and the caller's group handle is what ends them.
///
/// `launch-prepared-bounded` is `launch-bounded`'s limit behind `launch-prepared`'s gate. The live
/// bounded launch starts the program and is granted afterwards, which is the one launch shape a
/// failed grant could not roll back to "it never ran"; a prepared one is released only after the
/// same complete grant transaction as every other tool, and cancelled - record, Domain and all -
/// when that transaction fails.
///
/// AND A TRANSACTION IS A CONNECTION. Prepared state is keyed by the channel it was prepared on, and
/// a client's channel closing abandons everything it prepared - so a caller that has lost a reply
/// recovers by DROPPING the connection it made the call on rather than by asking again on it: the
/// late reply lands on a dead endpoint instead of being taken as the answer to the next call. A
/// pipeline is prepared, sealed and group-released over ONE connection for the same reason:
/// `release-group` requires every koid to be the caller's, and two connections are two callers.
interface process {
	@op(1) start:  func(name: string) -> result<process-info, error>;
	@op(2) list:   func() -> result<list<process-info>, error>;
	@op(3) launch: func(name: string, bootstrap: handle<channel>) -> result<start-result, error>;
	@op(4) launch-bounded: func(name: string, memory-limit: u64, bootstrap: handle<channel>) -> result<start-result, error>;
	@op(5) accounting: func() -> result<list<budget>, error>;
	@op(6) launch-prepared: func(name: string, bootstrap: handle<channel>) -> result<start-result, error>;
	@op(7) release: func(koid: koid) -> result<bool, error>;
	@op(8) release-group: func(@bound(8) koids: list<koid>) -> result<bool, error>;
	@op(9) cancel: func(koid: koid) -> result<bool, error>;
	@op(10) launch-prepared-bounded: func(name: string, memory-limit: u64, bootstrap: handle<channel>) -> result<start-result, error>;
	/// `launch-prepared-bounded` with EVERY limit rather than one, for a program whose footprint is
	/// stated in full - which is what a long-running service's is.
	///
	/// PREPARED AND NOT LIVE, because the limits have to be in place before the program runs and a
	/// live launch has already started it. Every one of the six is assigned to the Domain before the
	/// first thread is queued; if the Domain cannot be created or ANY assignment is refused, the
	/// launch fails and the Domain is torn down. There is no fallback to an unbounded launch: a
	/// service that came up outside its stated limits is one nothing would notice was outside them.
	@op(11) launch-prepared-limited: func(name: string, limits: resource-limits, bootstrap: handle<channel>) -> result<start-result, error>;
}

/// THE NARROW DOOR TO STOPPING THE MACHINE.
///
/// Before this, the Power key worked by handing its driver the root-Domain handle - and whoever
/// holds that can already `sys_domain_kill` the whole system and rewrite the root Domain's resource
/// limits. The kernel says so itself beside `sys_system_power`. So a keyboard driver held the
/// broadest capability in the system in order to answer Ctrl+Alt+Del, and so did the device manager
/// that passed it on and the supervisor that passed it to them.
///
/// Two ops and no arguments is the whole of what that path ever needed. The authority stays with
/// SystemManager, which is the one process resident for the life of the system; everything else
/// asks. A client of this interface can stop the machine and can do NOTHING ELSE, which is the
/// difference between a capability and a key to the building.
///
/// Deliberately not here: a graceful shutdown that stops services in order first. That is a
/// feature, this is an authority narrowing, and attaching one to the other delays the narrowing
/// behind work that has nothing to do with it.
interface system-power {
	/// Restart the machine. Returns only if the request was refused.
	@op(1) reboot: func() -> result<unit, error>;
	/// Power the machine off. Returns only if the request was refused.
	@op(2) power-off: func() -> result<unit, error>;
	/// A FORCED POWER-OFF DEADLINE, `seconds` from now: the kernel keeps the earliest one armed, never moves it later
	/// and never cancels it, and powers the machine off when it passes whatever the processes are doing. While one is
	/// armed every reset the kernel would make powers the machine off instead, and the sleep entry is refused. Armed
	/// before an orderly power-off whose end must not depend on the services it stops.
	@op(3) power-off-within: func(seconds: u32) -> result<unit, error>;
}

/// THE ORDERLY POWER-OFF, AND NOTHING ELSE: ServiceManager's sequence - every service stopped in reverse dependency order,
/// LogService's last batch flushed, then `system-power` - reached through this narrow interface rather than the shell's
/// admin channel, which can stop any service. Served by ServiceManager. A request while the sequence runs is answered as
/// already under way; one while a sleep's transaction runs ends the transaction at its next step, as the admin door does.
interface system-shutdown {
	@op(1) power-off: func() -> result<unit, error>;
}

/// What an orderly shutdown is about to do.
enum shutdown-action {
	power-off = 1,
	reboot    = 2,
}

/// THE ORDERLY SHUTDOWN'S NOTICE, beside `system-power` and not inside it: that interface's client can stop the
/// machine and nothing else, and this is the other direction - ServiceManager telling the services whose manifest
/// rows declare the notice what is coming, on the control channel it holds for each, all at once, and waiting
/// for their answers under one bound before its reverse-dependency kills. A service that does not answer in time
/// is killed with the rest. Immediate `system-power` calls stay notice-free by design.
interface shutdown-notice {
	@op(1) prepare: func(action: shutdown-action) -> result<unit, error>;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ sleep

/// A sleep the system can enter.
enum sleep-state {
	/// SUSPEND TO IDLE: the transaction with no firmware transition - every core parked, the timed wake the only
	/// timer.
	idle = 1,
	/// SUSPEND TO RAM: ACPI S3, the firmware's transition, memory kept in self-refresh.
	ram  = 2,
	/// HIBERNATION: the image written to disk and the machine off.
	disk = 3,
}

/// The steps of the suspend transaction, in order.
enum sleep-step {
	none     = 0,
	/// Every service whose manifest entry declares the sleep notice, told.
	announce = 1,
	/// The applications frozen.
	freeze   = 2,
	/// Every StorageService instance written back and holding new writes.
	flush    = 3,
	/// Every driver binding suspended.
	drivers  = 4,
	/// The platform prepared for the state: wake armed, `_PTS` and `_SST`.
	platform = 5,
	/// The kernel's entry.
	enter    = 6,
	/// Hibernation's image: its bindings resumed, the image written or discarded.
	image    = 7,
}

/// WHO ASKED FOR A SLEEP: an inhibitor delays an IDLE sleep by its bounded amount, and nothing else.
enum sleep-reason {
	/// A person, through a tool - `sleepctl`.
	requested     = 1,
	/// A sleep button, fixed or control-method.
	sleep-button  = 2,
	/// The lid closed.
	lid           = 3,
	/// The policy's idle timeout.
	idle          = 4,
	/// A critical battery, where hibernation is set up.
	critical      = 5,
	/// A thermal zone past `_HOT`, where hibernation is set up.
	thermal       = 6,
	/// A power button, fixed or control-method, set to suspend or hibernate.
	power-button  = 7,
}

/// How the last sleep ended.
enum sleep-outcome {
	/// Accepted and running now.
	running  = 1,
	/// Slept and woke.
	resumed  = 2,
	/// Refused before anything was frozen.
	refused  = 3,
	/// A step failed, and the steps already taken were unwound.
	unwound  = 4,
}

/// What woke the machine.
enum wake-reason {
	unknown      = 0,
	/// The sleep's own timed wake.
	timer        = 1,
	power-button = 2,
	sleep-button = 3,
	/// A device's interrupt the wake set holds.
	device       = 4,
	/// The CMOS alarm (S3's timed wake).
	rtc          = 5,
	/// The platform woke it and said nothing more (QEMU's `system_wakeup`).
	platform     = 6,
	/// HIBERNATION'S SNAPSHOT IS TAKEN: the machine did not sleep - its image is to be written.
	snapshot     = 7,
	/// RESTORED FROM A HIBERNATION IMAGE by a fresh boot.
	restored     = 8,
}

/// One core's wakeups while it was parked in a suspend to idle, by cause.
record core-park {
	cpu:    u32,
	timer:  u32,
	ipi:    u32,
	device: u32,
}

/// THE LAST SLEEP'S RECORD - what a requester reads after its request was accepted, since `suspend` answers at
/// acceptance and never at the resume.
record sleep-record {
	state:     sleep-state,
	reason:    sleep-reason,
	outcome:   sleep-outcome,
	/// The step that failed or refused, and who: a service or a binding by name, or the orderly sequence's door.
	step:      sleep-step,
	who:       string,
	why:       string,
	/// The boot-time clock at the request and the sleep's length, in nanoseconds; zero for a sleep that did not
	/// enter.
	requested: u64,
	slept:     u64,
	wake:      wake-reason,
	/// The device's interrupt identity, for `device`.
	wake-detail: u32,
	/// Each core's wakeups while parked, for a suspend to idle.
	@bound(64) cores: list<core-park>,
}

/// One source that can wake the machine, and whether it is armed.
record wake-source {
	/// `power button`, `sleep button`, `timed wake`, `scheduled wake`, or a device's companion node.
	name:   string,
	armed:  bool,
	detail: string,
}

/// One inhibition of an idle sleep, and how long it has left.
record sleep-inhibitor {
	reason:       string,
	remaining-ms: u64,
}

/// WHAT `sleepctl` SHOWS BESIDE THE LAST SLEEP: the states this machine's entry takes, the wake sources, the inhibitors
/// present, the cap a watchdog put on the last sleep and the scheduled wake.
record sleep-status {
	idle: bool,
	ram:  bool,
	disk: bool,
	@bound(32) wake-sources: list<wake-source>,
	@bound(16) inhibitors:   list<sleep-inhibitor>,
	/// The latest a watchdog let the last sleep last, in milliseconds from its entry; zero for no cap.
	watchdog-cap-ms: u64,
	/// The scheduled wake, as seconds since the Unix epoch; zero for none.
	scheduled-wake:  u64,
	/// HIBERNATION ON THIS MACHINE, as the image component says: set up, or why not - and, set up, the warning that limits
	/// it where no TPM seals the image's key; and what became of the image found at this boot - "none", or why it was
	/// refused. Not set up, with the reason, where the component does not run.
	hibernation-set-up: bool,
	hibernation-why:    string,
	last-image:         string,
}

/// THE AUTHORITY TO PUT THE MACHINE TO SLEEP - beside `system-power` and not inside it, because a client of that
/// interface can stop the machine and nothing else. Served by ServiceManager; clients reach it through manifest
/// roles and PermissionManager grants.
///
/// `suspend` AND `hibernate` ANSWER WHEN SERVICEMANAGER ACCEPTS OR REFUSES THE REQUEST, before any step runs and
/// without waiting on any participant - never at the resume: a requester may itself be a participant the
/// transaction waits on. Everything after acceptance is read from `last-sleep`.
interface system-sleep {
	/// Suspend to idle or to RAM, with a timed wake `timed-wake-ms` from now, or none for zero.
	@op(1) suspend:    func(state: sleep-state, timed-wake-ms: u64, reason: sleep-reason) -> result<unit, error>;
	/// HIBERNATE: the image written to the hibernation partition, then the machine off - or, `hybrid`, suspended to RAM
	/// with the image kept, so a battery that dies in the night loses nothing, and the image invalidated when the S3
	/// resumes. `unsupported` where the machine is not set up for it: no hibernation partition as large as memory, or no
	/// TPM to seal its key.
	@op(2) hibernate:  func(hybrid: bool, reason: sleep-reason) -> result<unit, error>;
	/// Delay an IDLE sleep by at most `milliseconds` - a download, a call. Nothing delays a lid, sleep-button or
	/// critical-battery sleep past its bound.
	@op(3) inhibit:    func(milliseconds: u32, reason: string) -> result<unit, error>;
	/// End this client's inhibition early.
	@op(4) release:    func() -> result<unit, error>;
	@op(5) last-sleep: func() -> result<sleep-record, error>;
	@op(6) status:     func() -> result<sleep-status, error>;
	/// A SCHEDULED WAKE - "wake the machine at 03:00" - for a connection minted with the `sleep-wake` grant alone: the
	/// next sleep's timed wake, a wall-clock alarm, as seconds since the Unix epoch; zero cancels it. It is spent by the
	/// sleep it wakes, and one whose time passed while the machine slept is spent once, not once per missed period. A
	/// suspend to RAM refuses a scheduled wake further than its alarm reaches.
	@op(7) schedule-wake: func(unix-seconds: u64) -> result<unit, error>;
}

/// THE SLEEP NOTICE, on the control channel ServiceManager holds for every service whose manifest entry declares
/// it - the one the shutdown notice travels on. `announce` at the transaction's first step; `hold-writes` and
/// `release-writes` to StorageService alone, once the applications are frozen and at the resume; `resumed` after
/// the drivers are back.
///
/// ITS OPERATIONS ARE NUMBERED APART from `shutdown-notice`'s and from every other interface a control channel carries, so
/// one channel can carry them all without a frame being read as another's.
interface sleep-notice {
	@op(16) announce:       func(state: sleep-state) -> result<unit, error>;
	@op(17) hold-writes:    func() -> result<unit, error>;
	@op(18) release-writes: func() -> result<unit, error>;
	@op(19) resumed:        func(state: sleep-state) -> result<unit, error>;
}

/// What DeviceManager answers for its step. `failed` is empty when every binding suspended; otherwise it names the
/// binding that refused or did not answer within its scaled bound, `why` says which, and every binding the step had
/// already suspended has been resumed - the step unwinds itself before it answers. On success, the companion nodes
/// of the bindings whose `SUSPENDED` said wake armed, and the latest time the machine must be awake by, for a device
/// that cannot be stopped.
record drivers-suspended {
	failed: string,
	why:    string,
	@bound(64) wake-nodes: list<string>,
	/// Milliseconds from the answer; zero for no bound.
	awake-by-ms: u64,
}

/// THE DRIVERS' STEP, asked of DeviceManager on the control channel ServiceManager holds for it. Each answer comes
/// when the step is done - one binding at a time, each under its own scaled bound - and DeviceManager keeps serving
/// meanwhile, so the answer is correlated rather than awaited in line.
interface device-sleep {
	/// THE BINDINGS THE SLEEP CANNOT SUSPEND, asked before anything is frozen: every `Online` binding whose entry
	/// declares no `suspend-deadline`, and every bind or stop in flight - each as its driver, its device and why.
	/// Empty when the sleep may go ahead.
	@op(20) check:   func() -> result<list<string>, error>;
	@op(21) suspend: func(state: sleep-state, arm-wake: bool, timed-wake-ms: u64) -> result<drivers-suspended, error>;
	/// Every suspended binding resumed; the answer names the devices that did not come back, which are being torn
	/// down and rebound.
	@op(22) resume:  func(state: sleep-state) -> result<list<string>, error>;
	/// HIBERNATION, AFTER ITS SNAPSHOT: the bindings the image is written through resumed - every one publishing a
	/// `block` or a `tpm` provider - and nothing else; the answer names them. The resume that follows resumes the rest.
	@op(23) resume-for-image: func() -> result<list<string>, error>;
	/// HYBRID SLEEP, ONCE THE IMAGE IS WRITTEN: the bindings `resume-for-image` resumed suspended again, for the suspend
	/// to RAM that follows.
	@op(26) suspend-image:    func() -> result<unit, error>;
	/// A RESTORE'S LAST STEP BEFORE MEMORY IS REPLACED: every binding of this boot stopped, its device quiet; answered
	/// once they are, naming any that did not stop within its bound.
	@op(27) stop-all:         func() -> result<list<string>, error>;
}

/// THE PLATFORM'S STEP, served by the ACPI service on the control channel ServiceManager holds for it: `prepare`
/// evaluates `_PRW` and `_DSW` or `_PSW` on the wake nodes, arms their wake GPEs, then runs `_PTS` and `_SST`;
/// `wake` runs `_WAK` and `_SST` and disarms what `prepare` armed.
interface platform-sleep {
	@op(24) prepare: func(state: sleep-state, @bound(64) wake-nodes: list<string>) -> result<unit, error>;
	@op(25) wake:    func(state: sleep-state) -> result<unit, error>;
}

/// THE APPLICATIONS' FREEZE, on ProcessService's supervisor root - which ServiceManager alone holds, and on which
/// every launch keeps the control-plane Domain and is never held.
interface application-freeze {
	@op(32) freeze: func() -> result<unit, error>;
	@op(33) thaw:   func() -> result<unit, error>;
}

/// What the kernel's entry answered.
record woke {
	wake:   wake-reason,
	detail: u32,
	/// The sleep's length, in nanoseconds.
	slept:  u64,
	@bound(64) cores: list<core-park>,
}

/// THE ENTRY, asked of SystemManager - the one holder of MANAGE on the root Domain, which the kernel's sleep entry
/// requires - by ServiceManager over the channel they already share. It answers when the machine is awake again.
interface sleep-entry {
	@op(28) enter: func(state: sleep-state, timed-wake-ms: u64) -> result<woke, error>;
	/// THE MACHINE OFF WITH ITS IMAGE WRITTEN: `\_S4` where the firmware offers it, soft-off otherwise. Answers only
	/// when it did not happen.
	@op(29) off-hibernated: func() -> result<unit, error>;
}

/// WHAT THE IMAGE COMPONENT SAYS OF THE MACHINE: whether hibernation is set up here, and why not; and what became of
/// the image it last found at a boot.
record hibernation-status {
	set-up:          bool,
	/// Why it is not set up - or, where it is, a warning that limits it (no TPM seals the image's key); empty for none.
	why:             string,
	partition-bytes: u64,
	memory-bytes:    u64,
	/// "none", "restored", or why an image found at boot was refused.
	last-image:      string,
}

/// THE IMAGE COMPONENT'S STEP, served by it on the control channel ServiceManager holds for it: `status` before a
/// hibernation is accepted, `write-image` once the snapshot is taken - the snapshot the kernel holds sealed, encrypted
/// and written, and given back - and `discard-image` when a hybrid sleep's S3 resumed and the image must not be.
interface hibernation-image {
	@op(30) status:        func() -> result<hibernation-status, error>;
	@op(31) write-image:   func() -> result<unit, error>;
	@op(34) discard-image: func() -> result<unit, error>;
}

/// A RESTORE'S DOOR, served by ServiceManager to the image component through a manifest client role: once every page of
/// an image is written into the kernel and its header invalidated, `replace` stops every binding of the fresh boot and
/// answers - and the component then replaces memory.
interface hibernation-restore {
	@op(1) prepare-replacement: func() -> result<unit, error>;
	/// NO IMAGE IS RESTORED - none was found, or it was refused, or its restore could not happen: the boot goes on, and
	/// what waited for the image's verdict (LogService's journal on the system volume) is let go.
	@op(2) boot-continues:      func() -> result<unit, error>;
}
